I have two suggestions/thoughts here closely tied to Instruction Reuse and Value Prediction
concepts mentioned here:

Trace Based Prefetch
--------------------
If you have a trace cache then record data lines that have been associated with that
trace along with a 2 bit prediction strength and use this to issue non binding prefetches

An extension of this idea is to have SW use inline caches for storing data prefetches
but recording catching mispredictions and patching is not cheap with SW initiated code
cache modifications.


Value Predictor Table (VPT)
---------------------------
Hashed on (PC + key) and returns value. The value could be value at an address or address.

Entries in this table are added by SW exposed instructions. It is essentially a hint from
SW saying if the PC and key matches theres a good chance the value will match too. Think of
it as runtime CSE.

The key in question can be either part of the pointer address or any other invariant that
SW can use. The general idea being if the input to the same code is the same, the data
used will also be the same. That may not always be the case, hence it needs to be left 
for SW to decide. 

If you think of it as a Result Cache, it would not be very far off!

Of course instructions speculated using VPT cannot commit until the keys have proved to be
correct. 

It might be useful to keep the key address current with coherence. So if the key address 
is written to else where invalidate the cache.


